ust start off with  talking about what is calprotectin  so calprotectin is a protein present in  leukocytes and it's a highly sensitive  marker of inflammation and so when the  leukocytes are activated they release  the calprotectin into the surrounding  area  and calprotectin binds to divalent metal  ions calcium magnesium and it's about to  sequester these metal ions from bacteria  and so we know that the leukocytes are  activated during this process  so when we're measuring calprotectin in  stool it's a highly sensitive marker for  intestinal inflammation  but it is not very specific because it's  just a general marker of inflammation so  cal protecting can be elevated for a  variety of different disease processes  so it's elevated in ibd and we're going  to talk about that in the next slide it  also can be elevated for certain cancers  or lesions or on invasive  gastroenteritis  and so it's not very specific for any  disease process but it helps to narrow  down for the clinician what might be  happening if there's an inflammatory  component happening in the intestine  and so for inflammatory bowel disease  ibd you might guess that cow protection  is elevated because it has an  inflammation component which is correct  so ibc is a non-infectious chronic  inflammation of the gi tract so the  immune system is overactive  activated for a variety of different  reasons  and it's attacking the gut and so  there's inflammation present  and with ibd the clinicians  put these or diagnoses into three  different categories so there's  ulcerative colitis and that affects  anywhere from the colon to the rectum  and it's more of the diffuse  inflammation of the inner mucosal layer  and then it could also be crohn's  disease and crohn's disease affects  anywhere in the entire gi tract from gum  to bomb as they like to say  and it's these discontinuous patchy  lesions that can affect a variety of the  tissue in the intestine  sometimes the clinician won't know  exactly which bucket the patient falls  in ulcerative colitis or crohn's disease  and so they might be diagnosed as  indeterminate and then the clinician  will collect more information and maybe  diagnose them later with ulcerative  colitis or crohn's disease  and for diagnosis usually what happens  is that the patient gets an endoscopic  procedure so that the gastroenterologist  can view the inflammation present  and they will collect tissues of the  intestine the biopsies  and bring the biopsies out and send them  for anatomical pathologists to view to  see if there is these characteristic  patterns of inflammation  and that's how it's diagnosed  so inflammatory bowel disease it's  not not very prevalent the problem is  about 0.4  in north america so it's pretty low but  it's a pretty serious disorder and we  want to catch it because it's chronic  and so we really need to start treating  these patients early and to get these  disease processes under control  um so  another uh scenario that looks like ibd  at least from the clinical symptoms that  is much higher that has a much higher  prevalence is irritable bowel syndrome  so here this is ibs the i stands for  something completely different irritable  bowel syndrome  and so this is a functional disorder  meaning that the gut is over or under  reactive for a variety of different  reasons  it's a clinical diagnosis so it's  diagnosed based on symptoms and it's  highly prevalent the prevalence is  estimated to be about 10 to 15 percent  maybe even higher in patients seeking a  gastroenterologist advice  and it's diagnosed by the room for  diagnostic criteria which is for current  abdominal pain at least one day per week  in the last three months associated with  at least two of the following criteria  related to defecation associated with a  stool change in frequency or in  appearance  and so a reason that this is kind of um  problematic is that you know patients go  to the gastroenterologist  they have abdominal pain they have  diar