nterologist or followed by their  provider just to make sure that  everything is is doing okay with this  patient they often have relapse and  remitting states of inflammation  and sometimes they might require just  different modes of treatment  um so here's a study and you can see  it's quite old it's a good one from 2010  where the these authors looked at 140  patients with crohn's disease  and 40 controls and they looked at the  simple endoscopic score for crohn's  disease versus the fecal cal protection  result  so the simple endoscopic score is using  a scope and looking at the  upper intestine or the lower intestine  and measuring how much inflammation is  present  and basically how to read this graph the  higher the number the more inflammation  is present and the more severity  and you can see that there's a nice  correlation with fecal calprotectin  results and the severity of the simple  endoscopic score for crohn's disease so  this is great because the fecal cal  protection result  is much  easier to do the patient can just submit  a stool sample get a result and the  clinician and the patient have some  indication of what's going on with the  patient  and so it's a great method for following  patients with ibd  and if you can compare it to other  things that are typically used to follow  these patients so here's kind of some  metrics on sensitivity and specificity  so using calprotectin above the cutoff  for normal or maybe borderline you can  see that the sensitivity is pretty good  for picking up a flare in inflammatory  bowel disease  once again the specificity isn't so  great but that's because of all the  things that we just talked about  you know people can have inflammation  present in their gi  tract for a variety of reasons and it's  important to rule out those other  reasons  if we compare it to other serum markers  that measure inflammation  crp and the white blood cell count you  can see that they have a much lower  sensitivity in detecting t