yne or  the extraction device so here are the  different bristol stool types so type  one we kind of think of as hard as rocks  my labs like to shake the container and  if it sounds like rocks they know it's  type one it should go to manual wayne  um the device can be used from types two  through six or most devices can  and so typically my lab will look at the  stool and see if if it's well  homogenized or if it's highly  heterogeneous and sort them to the  appropriate extraction method  and then type seven because  the extraction device just doesn't work  well with type seven always goes to the  manual wayne  and some issues with heterogeneous um  samples you know we're thinking of  undigested food mucus and fiber just  create problems with the cue with the  extraction device and we typically use  manual weighing  and then my lab also likes to collect  these little pill cans i think it's  pretty funny when they see these in the  stool sample and so that's that's some  entertainment for them  but there are other limitations for  stool as a matrix for quantitative  results um  mostly there's no normalization  for urine testing we often normalize to  creatinine the creatinine will give us a  hint if it's really dilute or really  concentrated and we can normalize  whatever analyte was tested in the urine  based on that there's no marker like  that for stool testing  and the water content varies so stool is  mostly water about 65 to 75 percent of  water  in the different bristol stool types  another interesting study found that the  average within subject coefficient of  variation is somewhere between 30 to 40  percent so what this means is that if a  healthy individual submits a stool  sample one day and then the next day  submits another stool sample  on average that cal potential result  will vary within 30 to 40 percent  and so this study estimated that a  reference change value would be about  100  to 130 percent  so what that means is that if a patient  submits a