r turnaround time to get the  results to the clinician  um and uh you know it's it's certainly  uh  of need because you know the volumes  probably are increasing steadily over  time  um some of the challenges you know for a  clinical chemistry lab  it's probably working with stool um but  you know the expertise probably exists  in your infectious disease laboratories  or your microbiology laboratories  and so it could be something that's done  in conjunction with those labs to kind  of work out the process and find  something that that suits them  great thank you  our next question  is there a liquid stool protocol or do  you always have to use the way method  for bristol 7 stool samples  yes um so with  um with my laboratory we use the whey  method  for the liquid stool samples because we  actually just use a transfer pipet and  we transfer some of the liquid sample  into a container that can be weighed on  the analytical balance and then we do  the certain dilutions  right now many of the package inserts  have a different process  where you can  pipette out a certain amount with a  pipette  right into the extraction device  my laboratories did test this but  because we  sort of sort the run specifically based  right now on the manual wayne and cue  set  we were getting used to our current  workflow and we really like it and so we  couldn't figure out how which which  bucket to put these liquid stool samples  in if we're doing a separate extraction  process from the two that we're already  doing  but i would say you know labs that have  more flexibility  in their workflow could probably figure  out how to do this with the pipette  method and it likely works  thank you  next question  what are if any concerns of false  positives or false negatives with this  assay  um  i mean so with false positives and false  negatives you're thinking more of like  what's happening in stool  um and so you know because it's an  immunoassay and it's measuring the  protein i don't know that we've really  seen any false positives or false  negatives but it's more thinking what's  happening to the patient you know this  fecal cal protecting could be increased  because maybe the patient is receiving  cancer treatment and they're having some  kind of intestinal inflammation  in that sense or potentially maybe fecal  calprotectin isn't increased because  maybe it's a slow amount of inflammation  higher in the upper or in the small  intestine and maybe the it's just too  dilute by the time it it comes out in  stool um and so it's important to think  about the clinical indications and why  this result might vary a little bit in  what would be expected  thank you  next question  the threshold is 50 but do i properly  understand that the variation is high  and the result can be 100 with another  extraction  um i mean so the the manufacturers set  the threshold for normal  when you repeatedly measure a stool  sample you will see some variation  within that stool sample  and that's your analytical variation  so what i was mentioning previously was  the reference change value the variation  within a patient  and so i would say that you know  laboratories have to set the  expectations for their analytical  variation um and a hundred would be  quite high because that's you know  that's quite a bit more stool to get  that result um and so i i think that  that's that's a little bit elevated  and it would be important to do studies  to kind of see what variation you would  expect  for your analytical side  thank  you it looks like we have time for one  more question  do you see fc testing volumes increasing  or decreasing in the future  this testing is actually steadily  increasing  you know the gastroenterologists really  like to get this result they  they find it very useful to know if the  patient has inflammation present in the  intestine because this kind of helps  them in their in their diagnostic  workups um and so we've seen this  steadily increasing um 