nterologist or followed by their  provider just to make sure that  everything is is doing okay with this  patient they often have relapse and  remitting states of inflammation  and sometimes they might require just  different modes of treatment  um so here's a study and you can see  it's quite old it's a good one from 2010  where the these authors looked at 140  patients with crohn's disease  and 40 controls and they looked at the  simple endoscopic score for crohn's  disease versus the fecal cal protection  result  so the simple endoscopic score is using  a scope and looking at the  upper intestine or the lower intestine  and measuring how much inflammation is  present  and basically how to read this graph the  higher the number the more inflammation  is present and the more severity  and you can see that there's a nice  correlation with fecal calprotectin  results and the severity of the simple  endoscopic score for crohn's disease so  this is great because the fecal cal  protection result  is much  easier to do the patient can just submit  a stool sample get a result and the  clinician and the patient have some  indication of what's going on with the  patient  and so it's a great method for following  patients with ibd  and if you can compare it to other  things that are typically used to follow  these patients so here's kind of some  metrics on sensitivity and specificity  so using calprotectin above the cutoff  for normal or maybe borderline you can  see that the sensitivity is pretty good  for picking up a flare in inflammatory  bowel disease  once again the specificity isn't so  great but that's because of all the  things that we just talked about  you know people can have inflammation  present in their gi  tract for a variety of reasons and it's  important to rule out those other  reasons  if we compare it to other serum markers  that measure inflammation  crp and the white blood cell count you  can see that they have a much lower  sensitivity in detecting those flare-ups  in patients with ibd  and then about the same specificity  and if we compare it to a clinical score  the crohn's disease activity index we  can see that that doesn't compare as  well as the lab results  and so the fecal cal protection results  because it's you know in close proximity  to the intestine it's actually one of  the best tests out there  used to follow patients with ibd  it's very helpful to put the information  in context with how the patient is  feeling  and and how they're doing with their  treatment  okay so we talked a little bit about the  clinical utility for fecal cal  protection screening for ibd versus ibs  and following patients with ibd  next let's talk about some of the  analytical considerations if for people  interested in bringing fecal cal  protection calprotectin in-house  so most fecal calprotectin assays are  some kind of amino acid format and maybe  they're an elisa and the eliza exists in  this plate format or maybe they're  kind of conjugated to this bead  and so if it's a plate format  usually that plate format is done in  batch  so it's done all at once  sometimes what can be problematic for  the batch format is that dilutions might  be have to repeat it on a second plate  so it might  cause a higher turnaround time for high  child protection results it's just  something to consider  all the steps for a manual elisa can be  accomplished manually so you don't have  to have special equipment and this is a  common method in a specialty lab and so  this is kind of a low entrance point for  laboratories thinking about bringing  this in-house  and maybe wanting to start with an elisa  format first  the other option that people can  consider for bringing in calprotectin  is the beads  and so the the amino acid is just  conjugated to the beads and the sandwich  eliza happens on the bead um and the  thing that's great about beads is that  it's random access you can pop the  specimen on the instrument and get the  results usually within 20 30 minutes  um the dilutions are performed on the  instrument so there's no waiting for the  three hour elisa to finish for the  dilution  um and the the the only sort of downfall  or maybe limitation is that it requires  the amino acid analyzer to perform that  assay for that specific vendor  and so those are just some things to  investigate if you're interested in  bringing fecal cal protectant in-house  and another thing that's important to  consider is the method of extraction so  extraction is the process of putting the  stool into some buffer and then  rigorously shaking it to extract the cow  protectant  from the stool  and so the gold standard is the manual  extraction process  and so what this  entails is that usually we do all of our  stool extractions within a hood  and so the technologist will stir the  stool because we like things well  homogenized and then we will mask out  usually about 100 migs of the stool  sample and then write down that specific  mass on that container and then dilute  it with an appropriate amount of buffer  and then that gets rigorously vortexed  and then it often has to undergo a  second dilution to be at the appropriate  concentration for the assay and it might  need a centrifugation  we're going to contrast that with the  device extraction  so the manufacturers have gotten very  creative and made many of these  extraction devices  and these are pre-filled tubes with  buffer and they have what we like to say  in my lab the magic wand  and the magic wand has these precision  grooves and these precision grooves  collects an accurate and precise amount  of stool and you can see i've i've just  shown some of the wands of different  extraction devices and just labeled how  much how many migs of stool they collect  and so it is much lower than the manual  weighing process  but the good thing about these devices  is that you know you poke it into a  homogenized dual sample stick it into  the device vortex it and then it's often  ready to go right into the assay at that  appropriate solution  so if we compare the two processes  so for manual wayne moore stool is used  and because of this it's often better  for highly heterogeneous dual samples  a manual wayne is also better for liquid  stool samples because if you can imagine  the liquid steel sample is going to drip  off the wand and that could be  problematic  the only issue with the manual wayne is  that it takes more time and effort  and if we compare that to the extraction  device  so for the extraction device less stool  is used  it's really great for more homogeneous  dual samples  and it's also a very efficient process  in extracting the stool  so within my lab we actually sort stool  samples by which extraction method they  should undergo and so if you look at the  bottom you can kind of see that i have  pictures of gravel shown  and so if there's lots of chunks in the  stool or it's highly heterogeneous then  that stool sample is going to be sorted  to one for manual wayne because we  probably need a larger stool sample to  collect the average um  average amount of what's all present to  get a more accurate cal protecting  results  whereas if you look more to the right  you can see that the gravel is more  finely and it's more  homogeneous  and so those kinds of steel samples are  more best for the device and so we sort  those into a run for the device  and we extract them in that way  how it usually pans out is we do about  sixty percent of our extractions with  the device and about forty percent of  our extractions for cal protection  through manual weighing so we actually  use both processes  because you know both are  are sort of beneficial for certain stool  samples  and one thing that labs always wonder is  is how should we monitor the entire  testing process for fecal cow protectant  and so one way that we do this in my lab  is that we have a pooled dual qc  which is exactly what it sounds like it  is cool stool that we try and target a  specific range of child protection and  it is well homogenized because we want  to use this qc sample for both manual  wayne and the device  and so we freeze a bunch of aliquots  into eppendorf tubes and every time the  lab sets up a batch of 50 samples 50  patient samples  they will include a pooled stool qc  and so this is monitoring both our  extraction process process and our  analytical process  and i've shown some data from  one of our our some of our early studies  these are the levi jennings plot for the  manual wayne and the device  and you can see that both perform very  well in sort of ensuring that this  testing process is tightly controlled  and both are about the same so as long  as the stool is homogeneous  both manners  methods of extraction work really well  it's the more heterogeneous tool samples  that we really need to focus on the  manual weighing for  um and so i would say you know perhaps  if your lab isn't running a ton of fecal  cow protection samples you don't want to  go to all this effort of making a pooled  stool qc and i totally get it because  you know it's not it it takes a little  bit for our lab to do it as well  um and so one one method that i could  see you know smaller labs doing is  finding a stool sample that is well  digested  that could be just easily homogenized  aliquoting that into eppendorf tubes  freezing it and using that as their  pooled qc extraction control throughout  the runs  and so these are just some suggestions  for how laboratories might want to  monitor their entire testing process  all right so we just went through some  of the analytical considerations  let's talk about the pre-analytical  considerations  and basically what i'm hinting at here  is that stool is just a very challenging  matrix and we we already talked about  this a little bit in the extraction  process  so in my laboratory i already told you  we sort of sort the stool types based on  if they can be done by manual wayne or  the extraction device so here are the  different bristol stool types so type  one we kind of think of as hard as rocks  my labs like to shake the container and  if it sounds like rocks they know it's  type one it should go to manual wayne  um the device can be used from types two  through six or most devices can  and so typically my lab will look at the  stool and see if if it's well  homogenized or if it's highly  heterogeneous and sort them to the  appropriate extraction method  and then type seven because  the extraction device just doesn't work  well with type seven always goes to the  manual wayne  and some issues with heterogeneous um  samples you know we're thinking of  undigested food mucus and fiber just  create problems with the cue with the  extraction device and we typically use  manual weighing  and then my lab also likes to collect  these little pill cans i think it's  pretty funny when they see these in the  stool sample and so that's that's some  entertainment for them  but there are other limitations for  stool as a matrix for quantitative  results um  mostly there's no normalization  for urine testing we often normalize to  creatinine the creatinine will give us a  hint if it's really dilute or really  concentrated and we can normalize  whatever analyte was tested in the urine  based on that there's no marker like  that for stool testing  and the water content varies so stool is  mostly water about 65 to 75 percent of  water  in the different bristol stool types  another interesting study found that the  average within subject coefficient of  variation is somewhere between 30 to 40  percent so what this means is that if a  healthy individual submits a stool  sample one day and then the next day  submits another stool sample  on average that cal potential result  will vary within 30 to 40 percent  and so this study estimated that a  reference change value would be about  100  to 130 percent  so what that means is that if a patient  submits a stool sample and it's 50  micrograms per gram  later on if they submit a second stool  sample you wouldn't consider the cal  protection result significantly  different unless it was at least 100  micrograms per gram so you need to see a  pretty big change in the calprotectin  results to see that it's significantly  different because of all this  variability  so that's kind of wrapping up my talk  just to kind of review the learning  objectives  so we talked about the pros and cons of  the quantitative measurements in stool  we discussed the extraction process and  i showed you some examples of using a  pooled stool qc to monitor the entire  testing process  and then we also discussed the best  ordering practices  or the clinical utility of fecal cal  protection testing that maximizes the  benefits for patients  so i'd like to thank csorn for  sponsoring this presentation with lab  roots and last of all i'd also like to  thank my lab for all the wonderful work  that they do and i think they do an  excellent job  and with that i'd like to also thank you  for your attention and i'm happy to  answer any questions  well thank you lisa for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  let's get started  our first question is can you summarize  the challenges and benefits for labs  wanting to bring fecal cal protectant  in-house  sure  i'd say that we've steadily seen a  growth in this assay over time  um and we also know that patients with  inflammatory bowel disease need to be  followed pretty closely by the  clinicians and so  usually in your local area area there  will be like an ibd clinic and and often  these patients are sending in samples  and so i think you know it benefits the  labs to bring this in-house because it's  a faster turnaround time to get the  results to the clinician  um and uh you know it's it's certainly  uh  of need because you know the volumes  probably are increasing steadily over  time  um some of the challenges you know for a  clinical chemistry lab  it's probably working with stool um but  you know the expertise probably exists  in your infectious disease laboratories  or your microbiology laboratories  and so it could be something that's done  in conjunction with those labs to kind  of work out the process and find  something that that suits them  great thank you  our next question  is there a liquid stool protocol or do  you always have to use the way method  for bristol 7 stool samples  yes um so with  um with my laboratory we use the whey  method  for the liquid stool samples because we  actually just use a transfer pipet and  we transfer some of the liquid sample  into a container that can be weighed on  the analytical balance and then we do  the certain dilutions  right now many of the package inserts  have a different process  where you can  pipette out a certain amount with a  pipette  right into the extraction device  my laboratories did test this but  because we  sort of sort the run specifically based  right now on the manual wayne and cue  set  we were getting used to our current  workflow and we really like it and so we  couldn't figure out how which which  bucket to put these liquid stool samples  in if we're doing a separate extraction  process from the two that we're already  doing  but i would say you know labs that have  more flexibility  in their workflow could probably figure  out how to do this with the pipette  method and it likely works  thank you  next question  what are if any concerns of false  positives or false negatives with this  assay  um  i mean so with false positives and false  negatives you're thinking more of like  what's happening in stool  um and so you know because it's an  immunoassay and it's measuring the  protein i don't know that we've really  seen any false positives or false  negatives but it's more thinking what's  happening to the patient you know this  fecal cal protecting could be increased  because maybe the patient is receiving  cancer treatment and they're having some  kind of intestinal inflammation  in that sense or potentially maybe fecal  calprotectin isn't increased because  maybe it's a slow amount of inflammation  higher in the upper or in the small  intestine and maybe the it's just too  dilute by the time it it comes out in  stool um and so it's important to think  about the clinical indications and why  this result might vary a little bit in  what would be expected  thank you  next question  the threshold is 50 but do i properly  understand that the variation is high  and the result can be 100 with another  extraction  um i mean so the the manufacturers set  the threshold for normal  when you repeatedly measure a stool  sample you will see some variation  within that stool sample  and that's your analytical variation  so what i was mentioning previously was  the reference change value the variation  within a patient  and so i would say that you know  laboratories have to set the  expectations for their analytical  variation um and a hundred would be  quite high because that's you know  that's quite a bit more stool to get  that result um and so i i think that  that's that's a little bit elevated  and it would be important to do studies  to kind of see what variation you would  expect  for your analytical side  thank  you it looks like we have time for one  more question  do you see fc testing volumes increasing  or decreasing in the future  this testing is actually steadily  increasing  you know the gastroenterologists really  like to get this result they  they find it very useful to know if the  patient has inflammation present in the  intestine because this kind of helps  them in their in their diagnostic  workups um and so we've seen this  steadily increasing um additionally you  know patients with inflammatory bowel  disease are learning how to read this  result and how to kind of follow their  disease process  and so that's very helpful for them to  make sure that their disease stays under  control  well thank you lisa do you have any  final comments for our audience  uh thank you all for attending and i'm  happy to answer any other questions that  you have so thank you  great well thank you again lisa for your  time today and your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor diasoren for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event until next time goodbye
 

